  data: {
    LLM: 'Game: Niu Niu (Ox-Ox / Bullfighting). Logic: See rules.objective and rules.gameplay.',
    Player: "Niu Niu (literally 'Ox Ox') is a popular gambling card game originating from Southern China and widely played in Southeast Asia. It is a banking game where players receive five cards and must split them into a three-card set (totaling a multiple of 10) and a two-card hand. The value of the two-card hand determines the strength of the 'Ox'. It is known for its fast pace, significant multipliers on high hands, and its ubiquity in Asian casinos and social gatherings.",
    objective: 'Beat the banker by forming a higher Niu value: split your five cards into a three-card set (sum must be a multiple of 10) and a two-card hand; the units digit of the two-card sum is your rank (0–9). Higher rank wins; Niu Niu (0) beats all point hands; ties are broken by highest single card.',
    gameplay: "One banker and multiple players. Each receives five cards. Players split their five cards into a three-card set (total must be a multiple of 10) and a two-card hand. The two-card hand's point value (units digit only, e.g. 17 counts as 7) is the hand rank. Banker does the same. Compare each player's hand to the banker: higher point value wins. Niu Niu (both three- and two-card parts total zero) is the highest normal hand. Ties broken by highest single card. Equal bet pays 1:1 (minus 5% commission on wins); Double bet pays 1:1 for 1-6 points, 2:1 for 7-9, 3:1 for Niu Niu (player can lose up to 3x on Double).",
    keyRules: [
      'Split five cards into three-card (multiple of 10) and two-card hand; units digit of two-card sum is your Niu value (0-9).',
      'Higher Niu value beats lower; Niu Niu (0+0) beats all point hands. Tie-break by highest card.',
      'Equal bet: 1:1 win/loss, 5% commission on wins. Double bet: 1-6 pays 1:1, 7-9 pays 2:1, Niu Niu pays 3:1; lose up to 3x stake.',
      'Face cards count as 10. Aces typically 1 or 11 by variant.',
    ],
